\title{BFS is a test for bipartiteness}
\date{2026-09-23T03:48:53Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\taxon{Remark}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{The above proof does much more than just establish the truth of the
theorem. It actually describes a practical algorithmic \em{test} for
bipartiteness. Run a BFS starting from any vertex; then #{G} is
bipartite iff there is no edge between two vertices in the same
layer. If there is a cross-edge within a layer, then we have found an
odd cycle; otherwise, we have found a 2-coloring of the graph.}

\p{That is, this algorithm tells us not only whether a graph is
bipartite, but gives us concrete (and easily checkable) \em{evidence}
to support the conclusion: either an odd cycle, showing the graph is
not bipartite, or a specific partition of the vertices showing that it is.}

